    #### Local A3 nightly validation (2026-10-15; frozen head `444e962`)
    
    These four model runs used **`444e962b239c3a8fc56b9255d601818e6e028b9a`
    + vLLM 0.31.0 (`db9527a`)**, which is also the restored PR head; the
    deferred connector hardening is not included. Prefill 138 + Decode 136,
    16 NPUs per node; original image, weights, datasets, launch and
    benchmark parameters were retained, with only the second-node address
    mapped from 139 to 136. All four cases exited pytest 0 on both nodes
    (one passed JUnit case per node). OTT is output token/s; TPOT/TTFT are
    ms.
    
    | Case | Performance requests | OTT / required minimum | TPOT / TTFT |
    AIME2025 accuracy | Result |
    | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
    | Kimi-K2.6 PD | 60/60 | 355.1593 / 337.024075 | 20.5 / 3268.2 | 90%,
    within 93.33±10 | Performance + accuracy passed |
    | DeepSeek-V4 Flash PD-prefix | Accuracy only | N/A | N/A | 90%, within
    90±10 | Accuracy passed |
    | GLM-5.1 W8A8C8 128K | 256/256 | 811.7645 / 282.105876 | 39.8 / 32665.6
    | No accuracy item | Performance passed |
    | MiniMax-M2.7 PD-prefix | 160/160 | 711.0518 / 698.497 | 34.8 / 17438.2
    | 90%, within 93.33±10 | Performance + accuracy passed |
    
    All performance requests above, and the valid controls below, were
    checked for actual 1024-token outputs, not just success flags.
    
    **Conditional controls:** historical throughput differences triggered
    only the affected performance cases against the same PR base `6d1dd70`,
    without this PR, + vLLM 0.30.0 (`ced6857`). Kimi: 355.1593 vs 359.8514
    OTT (**-1.30%**); the earlier 7.27% loss was not reproduced in this
    comparison. TPOT was 20.5 vs 19.7ms (+0.8ms/+4.06%), and average request
    latency +3.48%; a single pair does not establish latency equivalence or
    a stable regression. GLM: 811.7645 vs 813.3506 OTT (**-0.20%**), TPOT
    39.8 vs 39.4ms. Kimi's original random request pacing affects aggregate
    OTT. No alternating reruns or unaffected-model 0.30 controls were added.
    
    Archived evidence:
    `main2main-nightly-20261013/pr18229-update-444e962-20261015/`
    (`FINAL_REPORT.md`, per-node logs/JUnit, raw AISBench outputs,
    token-count audit, controls and diagnostic evidence). Both nodes were
    released after testing.
    
    #### Deferred follow-up: multi-group receive-failure reporting (not
    included)
    
    The request-level receive-failure hardening change has been removed from
    this upgrade PR; the branch is restored to the nightly-tested head
    `444e962`. The original four nightlies already passed on this head
    **without** that fix, so it is not required for these normal-path
    workloads to pass.
    
    The fault-path issue remains real: if a multi-group receive actually
    fails, the existing connector can report flat block IDs and trigger the
    vLLM 0.31 scheduler guard, terminating Decode EngineCore. This is
    handled separately in
    [#18823](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm-ascend/pull/18823),
    retaining ordinary non-HMA block-level recovery semantics. The upgrade
    PR does not claim fault isolation is fixed.
    
    Development fault-injection results belong to the deferred connector
    implementation, not this restored head. No new NPU run was performed
    when restoring the exact previously tested code.
    
